UPDATE "QuestionBankItem" SET options = $o1157${"options": ["Internuclear ophthalmoplegia", "Homonymous hemianopia, as a description of that mechanism alone", "Facial nerve palsy only", "Cerebellar ataxia only, as a description of that mechanism alone"], "distractorRationale": {"Homonymous hemianopia, as a description of that mechanism alone": "Homonymous hemianopia results in loss of vision in half of the visual field, which does not account for the specific eye movement abnormalities seen in this patient. (This option fails because it does not address the **adduction impairment** that characterizes internuclear ophthalmoplegia.)", "Facial nerve palsy only": "Facial nerve palsy affects the muscles of facial expression and does not involve eye movements, making it irrelevant to the patient's symptoms of double vision and impaired adduction. (This option is incorrect as it does not relate to the **neurological coordination** of eye movements.)", "Cerebellar ataxia only, as a description of that mechanism alone": "Cerebellar ataxia primarily affects coordination and balance, not specifically eye movements, which are crucial in this case. (This option is misleading because it overlooks the specific **eye movement abnormalities** indicative of internuclear ophthalmoplegia.)"}, "clinicalReasoning": "The patient's symptoms are consistent with internuclear ophthalmoplegia due to a lesion in the medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF). **Impaired adduction** of the right eye during lateral gaze indicates a specific neurological issue. Internuclear ophthalmoplegia is often caused by **lesions** in the MLF, affecting eye coordination.", "keyTakeaways": ["When a patient presents with impaired adduction of the eye during lateral gaze, think internuclear ophthalmoplegia first."]}$o1157$::text,
